[h1]R.A.T.S. Cities and Borders[/h1]
[i]R.A.T.S. – Reconnaissance, Acoustics, Terrain & Signals[/i]
[b]Bugs, ideas, questions:[/b] [url=https://hub.sewerlabs.dev/s/borders]R.A.T.S. feedback board[/url]

[b]The stock map shows coastlines. Now it shows the world as it was on the day of your mission.[/b]
National borders, capitals, cities and naval bases of the mission's date: the Soviet Union, East and West Germany, Yugoslavia, Leningrad and Bonn, Holy Loch and Cam Ranh Bay.

[b]It only draws on the map. Nothing in the game or in a mission changes, and the lines are a reference map, not the rules of your mission.[/b] Read "What the lines are and aren't" below before you trust a line in a tense mission.

[h2]The highlights[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Borders by date.[/b] The Soviet Union is one country until the Baltic states split off in September 1991 and the rest in December 1991. The inner-German border and West Berlin until October 1990, Yugoslavia until 1991/92, Czechoslovakia until 1993, North and South Yemen until May 1990, the Vietnam DMZ until 1976
[*][b]Capitals and names of the time.[/b] East Berlin and Bonn before October 1990, Leningrad, Gorky, Karl-Marx-Stadt, Bombay, Peking before 1979
[*][b]Naval bases with an anchor[/b] in their side's colour, blue for NATO and its allies, red for the Warsaw Pact, plus air bases, only in the years they were used: Holy Loch until 1992, Cam Ranh Bay as a Soviet base 1979 to 2002
[*][b]Disputed lines dashed.[/b] Korea, Kashmir, the Golan, Cyprus
[*][b]In the mission editor too,[/b] for the date you set there: two buttons at the top left of the editor map, F7 and Shift+F7
[*]An optional coastal distance line (3, 6 or 12 nm off the coast), off by default
[*]Lines keep their width at every zoom, names don't overlap, a lighter colour set for the night map, and no doubled names where the mission already marks a place
[/list]

[h2]How to use[/h2]
Open the map in a mission, or the map of the mission editor. The layers are in the map menu (≡), Map Layers, [b]Borders[/b] and [b]Cities and bases[/b], or use Shift+F7 for borders and F7 for cities. The coastal distance line stays off until you set [i]Coastal distance line (nm)[/i] to 3, 6 or 12 in the config.

[h2]What the lines are and aren't[/h2]
[list]
[*]The mod doesn't touch rules of engagement, triggers, zones or scoring, and doesn't change what your units see or do
[*]Missions set their own areas and rules, and those often don't match real geography. [i]Northern Vigil[/i] goes weapons free when two Soviet vessels enter its own 10 nm circle, [i]Don't Mess With Texas[/i] fails you for attacking an airbase, [i]Operation Trident[/i] has an exclusion zone off Karachi. Go by the briefing and the mission's own map areas (Map Layers, On Map Areas), not by anything this mod draws
[*]A ship crossing one of these lines is not a violation in the game unless the mission says so, and the same goes the other way round
[*]The borders are today's lines with date rules on top, simplified; disputed ones are drawn the way Natural Earth draws them. Land borders only: no sea borders, airspace or zones. The Berlin Wall and the Vietnam DMZ are hand-drawn and approximate
[*]The coastal distance line is not a territorial limit. It is one distance from the game's coastline, the same for every country; in 1985 Norway claimed 4 nm, the US and the UK 3, the Soviet Union 12. Treat it as a ruler
[*]Cities, capitals and bases come from the game's own lists plus public data. A base on the map doesn't mean the mission models it. Units and contacts are exactly what the game shows
[/list]

[h2]Where the data comes from[/h2]
[list]
[*]Land borders: [url=https://www.naturalearthdata.com/]Natural Earth[/url] 1:10m boundary lines, public domain (release 5.1.2). Which border existed when is my own work
[*]Inner-German border, North/South Yemen and West Berlin: built from Natural Earth's state lines. Berlin Wall and Vietnam DMZ: hand-drawn
[*]Cities, ports and airfields: the game's campaign lists, read from your install when a mission starts. None of the game's data is copied or shipped
[*]More cities and capitals: Natural Earth populated places, public domain (today's populations, so the 1985 ranking is a bit off)
[*]Naval bases with side and years: my own list, positions approximate
[*]Coastal distance line: worked out in the game from its own world grid
[/list]
Made with Natural Earth. Free vector and raster map data at naturalearthdata.com.

[h2]What may come[/h2]
Borders and sea limits drawn the way each country defined them back then, instead of one distance for everybody. It would still be a reference, not mission rules. Ideas and corrections are welcome in the comments.

[h2]Settings[/h2]
[i]<Sea Power>\BepInEx\config\dev.sewerlabs.seapowerborders.cfg[/i]. Borders: toggle key, era (mission date or today), disputed lines, line width, opacity, coastal distance line. Cities: toggle key, density, naval bases, air bases, civil airports, label size.

[h2]Install and game versions[/h2]
[b]R.A.T.S. Keel[/b] is required: [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3835527459]subscribe[/url] and switch it on in the Mods menu; its page has the one install step. It replaces Anchor Chain.
Run in the game on the 3D globe public beta (build 261107), loaded by R.A.T.S. Keel, and earlier versions on Sea Power 0.8.7 with Topographic Map, Better TacMap, R.A.T.S. Radio and R.A.T.S. Pop-Out Windows loaded. The package has a build for each game and the right one switches on by itself; leave both DLLs in the folder. No game files are changed. If a game update breaks one of the mod's hooks, it says so in the log and stays out of the way.

[h2]Problems, feedback, ideas[/h2]
Post bugs, feedback and ideas on the [url=https://hub.sewerlabs.dev/s/borders]R.A.T.S. feedback board[/url]. For a bug, add [i]<Sea Power>\BepInEx\LogOutput.log[/i] (as a link) and tell me the mission and its start date.

Source code (MIT): [url=https://github.com/struckl/SeaPowerBorders]github.com/struckl/SeaPowerBorders[/url]

